//! core/host/testing — the test runner's platform, one double per effect.
//!
//! `core/host` is importable only from the module that exports `main`; this is
//! the same surface for a test source, and the `testing` segment in the path is
//! the whole of that rule (SPEC 4.1.1). The two restrictions are the same
//! shape, so between them they still name every place in a program where
//! authority can enter.
//!
//! Every name here is `core/host`'s name — `alloc`, `stdout`, `stderr`,
//! `stdin`, `fs`, `net`, `clock`, `rand`, `env`, `proc` — but **called** rather
//! than referred to.
//! `core/host`'s `clock` is one clock because a process has one; `clock()` is a
//! fresh one every call, so what one test does to it is invisible to the next.
//! That is the whole reason these are functions and `core/host`'s are `let`s.
//!
//! Configuration is a **method on the value that answers a new handle**:
//! `clock().at(1000)`, `rand().seed(7)`,
//! `env().variables([...]).arguments([...])`.
//! There are no free `clockAt`/`randSeed`/`envOf` constructors, because a
//! reader who knows `clock()` should not have to learn a second name to start
//! it somewhere else, and because a chain reads in the order it is applied.
//!
//! Most structs carry an `I64` handle rather than their state. A captured
//! stdout accumulates what a test writes to it and Buri has no mutation, so the
//! state lives on the runner's side and the value here names it —
//! `backend/js/runtime.js`'s `$t.h` on one backend and
//! `cli/runtime/testing.rs`'s `TABLE` on the other, which is one design written
//! twice because the *program* is what has to behave identically.
//!
//! `TestNet` is the exception and the exception is the rule read carefully: its
//! configuration is a **responder**, which is behaviour rather than state, and
//! behaviour is precisely what a runner written in Rust and JavaScript cannot
//! hold on a program's behalf. It carries the function itself, `fetch` is a
//! Buri body, and no runtime table names `fetch` at all. `TestNet`'s own
//! documentation has the two reasons in full. It carries a handle beside the
//! responder, and that is the same rule rather than an exception to it: the
//! handle names its **log**, and a log is state.
//!
//! `TestFs` is the second value that is not a bare handle, and the second
//! reason is the first one read again. A fault plan holds `IoError` *values* and
//! `IoError.Other` carries a `Str`, which `cli/runtime/lib.rs` §2.1 has no way
//! to hand back across a row — so the plan travels in the program, the eleven
//! methods of `Fs` are Buri bodies over rows that take the handle, and the
//! runner holds what it can: the log, the files, and which entries of the plan
//! have fired.
//!
//! ## The call log
//!
//! `TestFs`, `TestNet` and `TestStdin` each keep every call made through the
//! handle, and `calls()` answers them **in the order they completed**. What a
//! test asserts on is still the outcome — the return value, `snapshot()`,
//! `captured()` — and the log is for the assertions those cannot make: that a
//! cache was consulted once and not twice, that nothing was written at all,
//! that a retry went to the same URL. One `Call` record per double, built by a
//! constructor named after the method it records, compared by `Eq` and printed
//! by `Show`.
//!
//! `TestProc` is the other type with no state, for the plainer reason: it has
//! nothing to read back. `exitWith` absorbs the call so that a test which
//! exits keeps running, and what a test asserts is what it does *after* the
//! exit — so there is nothing left for the runner to hold, and no row either.
//!
//! ## The fault plan
//!
//! `fs().faults([...])` and `net().faults([...])` say what breaks. A fault is
//! one of those same `Call` constructors and an error — `readFile(p).fails(e)`
//! for every matching call, `readFile(p).failsOnCall(n, e)` for the `n`th — and
//! the match is the `Eq` the `Call` records already derive, so a fault is
//! spelled exactly as `calls()` reports the call it names. **Success comes from
//! the environment and failure comes from the plan**: `files` and `respond` say
//! what a call finds, and nothing else in the module says what a call answers.
//!
//! **A fault whose call never happens fails the test.** That is a claim the
//! program cannot check for itself — the block has returned by the time anyone
//! could — so the runner checks it: `buri_rt_test_leave(index)` runs after every
//! `test` body and reports the plans declared inside it that have entries
//! nothing fired. It is the reason `TestFs` and `TestNet` each carry two things,
//! a handle and a plan, and the reason `faults` hands the runner a rendering of
//! each entry as it installs it.
//!
//! ## The order the work happens in
//!
//! `tasks()` is the one double whose subject is not state but **scheduling**.
//! `Tasks.parallel` promises its results in the items' order and promises
//! nothing about the order the work runs in, so a program that depended on the
//! second would pass every test and fail in production. This double makes the
//! order a value a test writes down: program order by default, `anyOrder()` for
//! one seeded order, and `everyOrder()` for all of them — which re-runs the
//! `test` body once per completion order, so the assertion at the end of it is
//! an assertion about every order rather than about one. A seed is the order's
//! own number, so a failure names a line that replays it.
//!
//! ## `arguments`, and the name that had to move to make room for it
//!
//! Every builder here is spelled exactly as the design note writes it, and
//! `arguments` is the one that cost something. A type's methods are one map
//! keyed by name — an `impl Env for TestEnv` method registers into it through
//! the same call an inherent one does — so the arity and the return type
//! separate nothing, and `Env`'s reader and this builder cannot both be
//! `arguments`. Behind that they would mangle to one intrinsic key and one C
//! symbol in each of the two runtime tables.
//!
//! So **the reader moved**: `core/effect`'s `Env` declares `args(self): [Str]`
//! now, which is the name a program already used — `core/env` has always
//! exported `args(ctx)` and was the only caller of the method — and
//! `TestEnv.arguments([Str])` is the builder. The reader is on the effect and
//! the builder is on the double, so the two never meet on one type anyway;
//! what they were competing for was the name, and the name went to the surface
//! a test writes.

from "core/effect/lib.buri" import {
  Alloc, Clock, Env, Fs, Header, IoError, Method, Net, NetError, Proc, Rand,
  Region, Request, Response, Stderr, Stdin, Stdout, Tasks,
};

// --- Alloc --------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Real, from a per-test arena the runner reclaims.
///
/// The one double with no state at all: the charge is a function of the *types*
/// (`core/effect`'s `Alloc`), so `allocate` answers the byte count it was asked
/// for and the handle names nothing. Both native backends open-code it for
/// exactly that reason.
export struct TestAlloc(I64);
impl Alloc for TestAlloc {
  fn allocate(self, bytes: Int): Region;
}
export fn alloc(): TestAlloc;

// --- Output -------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Captured, and never printed. `captured` is the only way to read it back,
/// which is what makes a test's output something to assert on rather than
/// something to watch scroll past — a failing test says so through its
/// assertion.
export struct TestStdout(I64);
impl Stdout for TestStdout {
  fn print(self, text: Template): ();
  fn println(self, text: Template): ();
  /// Captured as the UTF-8 text the octets spell, so `captured` answers one
  /// question rather than two.
  fn writeBytes(self, b: [U8]): ();
}
export fn stdout(): TestStdout;

impl TestStdout {
  /// Everything written to this stream so far.
  export fn captured(self): Str;
}

/// The same, for standard error.
export struct TestStderr(I64);
impl Stderr for TestStderr {
  fn eprint(self, text: Template): ();
  fn eprintln(self, text: Template): ();
}
export fn stderr(): TestStderr;

impl TestStderr {
  /// Everything written to this stream so far. The same name `TestStdout`
  /// carries: two types, one question, and the receiver says which stream.
  export fn captured(self): Str;
}

// --- Input --------------------------------------------------------------------

/// End of input, until a test says otherwise: `readLine` and `readBytes` both
/// answer `.None`.
///
/// `core/host`'s `stdin` is the process's, which a test must never read — a
/// suite that blocked on a pipe nobody was writing to would hang rather than
/// fail. This one is a fixture, and the default fixture is the empty one.
export struct TestStdin(I64);
impl Stdin for TestStdin {
  fn readLine(self): Option<Str>;
  fn readBytes(self, n: Int): Option<[U8]>;
}
export fn stdin(): TestStdin;

impl TestStdin {
  /// A **new** stdin reading those lines, then end of input.
  export fn lines(self, lines: [Str]): TestStdin;
  /// A **new** stdin reading those octets, then end of input.
  ///
  /// `lines` and `bytes` **replace** each other rather than composing the way
  /// `TestEnv`'s two builders do, and that is the difference between two axes
  /// and one: a stream is either the lines a test wrote or the octets it wrote,
  /// and a stdin built from octets answers `.None` to `readLine`. The last
  /// builder in the chain is the stream.
  export fn bytes(self, b: [U8]): TestStdin;
  /// Every read made through this handle, in the order they completed.
  ///
  /// A read that ran off the end of the stream is a call like any other: the
  /// log records what the code under test asked for, not what it was given.
  export fn calls(self): [StdinCall];
}

// --- Filesystem ---------------------------------------------------------------

/// In-memory and empty. Writes are visible to that test and discarded after it.
///
/// A file holds **octets**, and `readFile` decodes them as UTF-8 the way a real
/// filesystem's does — so `writeFileBytes` of a byte a text encoding cannot
/// carry reads back through `readFileBytes` unchanged.
///
/// Two places where an in-memory filesystem is thinner than a real one, both
/// observable and both deliberate. It is a flat map of paths, so the parents of
/// a fixture's path are not directories until `makeDir` says so; and `sync` has
/// nothing to flush, so it is `.Ok` for any path that exists. Everything else —
/// append creating a file, rename replacing one, removing what is not there —
/// answers exactly what a real filesystem answers, which is what lets one
/// conformance block run against both.
///
/// ## Two fields, for `TestNet`'s reason
///
/// `handle` names the runner-side state — the files, the attenuation, the log,
/// and the plan's own bookkeeping — and `faults` is the **plan itself**, which
/// stays in the program. A fault carries an `IoError` and `IoError.Other`
/// carries a `Str`: `cli/runtime/lib.rs` §2.1 names an error variant by its
/// index and restricts that variant to carrying no fields, so a row could not
/// hand the value back even if the runner held it. So the plan is a Buri value,
/// the match is the `Eq` the `FsCall` records derive, and every method here is a
/// body that asks the plan before it calls its row. What the runner keeps is
/// what a failure message needs and which entries have fired, which is the one
/// half of `faults` a program cannot remember for itself.
export struct TestFs {
  handle: I64,
  plan: [FsFault],
}

impl Fs for TestFs {
  fn readFile(self, path: Str): Result<Str, IoError> {
    match (self.planned(readFile(path))) {
      .Some(error) => .Err(error),
      .None => fsReadFile(self.handle, path),
    }
  }
  fn writeFile(self, path: Str, body: Str): Result<(), IoError> {
    match (self.planned(writeFile(path, body))) {
      .Some(error) => .Err(error),
      .None => fsWriteFile(self.handle, path, body),
    }
  }
  /// The one method of `Fs` that cannot fail, and so the one no fault can ever
  /// fire on. A plan naming it is a plan whose call never happens, which the
  /// post-condition reports at the end of the block.
  fn fileExists(self, path: Str): Bool {
    fsFileExists(self.handle, path)
  }
  fn readDir(self, path: Str): Result<[Str], IoError> {
    match (self.planned(readDir(path))) {
      .Some(error) => .Err(error),
      .None => fsReadDir(self.handle, path),
    }
  }
  fn readFileBytes(self, path: Str): Result<[U8], IoError> {
    match (self.planned(readFileBytes(path))) {
      .Some(error) => .Err(error),
      .None => fsReadFileBytes(self.handle, path),
    }
  }
  fn writeFileBytes(self, path: Str, body: [U8]): Result<(), IoError> {
    match (self.planned(writeFileBytes(path, body))) {
      .Some(error) => .Err(error),
      .None => fsWriteFileBytes(self.handle, path, body),
    }
  }
  fn appendFile(self, path: Str, body: [U8]): Result<(), IoError> {
    match (self.planned(appendFile(path, body))) {
      .Some(error) => .Err(error),
      .None => fsAppendFile(self.handle, path, body),
    }
  }
  fn renameFile(self, source: Str, destination: Str): Result<(), IoError> {
    match (self.planned(renameFile(source, destination))) {
      .Some(error) => .Err(error),
      .None => fsRenameFile(self.handle, source, destination),
    }
  }
  fn removeFile(self, path: Str): Result<(), IoError> {
    match (self.planned(removeFile(path))) {
      .Some(error) => .Err(error),
      .None => fsRemoveFile(self.handle, path),
    }
  }
  fn makeDir(self, path: Str): Result<(), IoError> {
    match (self.planned(makeDir(path))) {
      .Some(error) => .Err(error),
      .None => fsMakeDir(self.handle, path),
    }
  }
  fn syncFile(self, path: Str): Result<(), IoError> {
    match (self.planned(syncFile(path))) {
      .Some(error) => .Err(error),
      .None => fsSyncFile(self.handle, path),
    }
  }
}

export fn fs(): TestFs {
  TestFs { handle: newFs(), plan: [] }
}

impl TestFs {
  /// A **new** filesystem holding this one's files and these as well, as the
  /// UTF-8 the text spells. The last entry for a path wins, and so does a later
  /// builder: `fs().files(a).files(b)` is `a` overwritten by `b`.
  export fn files(self, entries: [(Str, Str)]): TestFs {
    TestFs { handle: fsFiles(self.handle, entries), plan: self.plan }
  }
  /// The byte twin, for a fixture that is not text. It composes with `files` in
  /// either order, because both write into the one map a file lives in.
  export fn filesBytes(self, entries: [(Str, [U8])]): TestFs {
    TestFs { handle: fsFilesBytes(self.handle, entries), plan: self.plan }
  }
  /// A **new** handle onto the *same* files, through which every write fails
  /// with `.ReadOnly`.
  ///
  /// This is `core/testing/context`'s `ReadOnly<C>` folded into a method, and
  /// the fold keeps what made the wrapper a wrapper: it holds the inner value
  /// rather than a copy of it, so a read through the attenuated handle sees
  /// whatever the filesystem holds *now*. `readFile`, `fileExists`, `readDir`,
  /// `readFileBytes` and `syncFile` are forwarded — `sync` is not a write and
  /// there is nothing this could be hiding — and the six that write are
  /// refused.
  ///
  /// `ReadOnly<C>` itself stays: it attenuates *any* `Fs`, including one a test
  /// wrote, and this method attenuates only this one.
  export fn readOnly(self): TestFs {
    TestFs { handle: fsReadOnly(self.handle), plan: self.plan }
  }
  /// A **new** filesystem onto the same files, failing the calls this plan
  /// names — and a promise that every one of them is made.
  ///
  /// A fault is a call and an error: `readFile(p).fails(e)` fails every matching
  /// call, and `readFile(p).failsOnCall(n, e)` fails the `n`th, counted from one
  /// over the matching calls made through this handle. Matching is the `Eq` the
  /// `FsCall` records derive, so a fault names a call exactly as `calls()`
  /// reports it — same constructor, same arguments.
  ///
  /// **Success comes from the environment and failure comes from the plan.**
  /// `files` says what a read finds; this says what fails. A call the plan fails
  /// is not performed — nothing is written and nothing is removed — and it is
  /// still a call, so `calls()` has it.
  ///
  /// **A fault whose call never happens fails the test.** A plan is a claim
  /// about what the code under test does, and a claim nothing exercised is one
  /// the next change to that code will quietly stop being true. The runner
  /// checks it at the end of the block, so an unconsumed fault is a failure with
  /// the fault's own spelling in it rather than a test that passed.
  ///
  /// `faults` **replaces** rather than composing, as `respond` does and for the
  /// same reason: two plans for one call have no answer to which of them wins.
  /// The plan it replaced is retired, and its promise with it.
  export fn faults(self, plan: [FsFault]): TestFs {
    let handle = fsWithPlan(self.handle);
    let _ = describeFsPlan(handle, plan, 0);
    TestFs { handle: handle, plan: plan }
  }
  /// What this filesystem holds at `path`, or `.Err(.NotFound)`.
  ///
  /// The same answer `readFile` gives, reachable without the `Fs` effect: a
  /// test asserting on what a function *wrote* is reading an environment back
  /// rather than performing an effect, and should not have to bind one to do
  /// it. A read-back is not a call, and no fault fires on one.
  export fn read(self, path: Str): Result<Str, IoError> {
    fsRead(self.handle, path)
  }
  /// Every file, as text, **sorted by path**.
  ///
  /// Sorted rather than in the order the writes happened, because the order of
  /// the writes is `calls()`'s question and the contents is this one's: a test
  /// that asserted on insertion order would fail the day a function under test
  /// reordered two writes that do not interact.
  ///
  /// A directory `makeDir` created holds no octets and is not a file, so it is
  /// not here; `readDir` is the question it answers.
  export fn snapshot(self): [(Str, Str)] {
    fsSnapshot(self.handle)
  }
  /// Every call made through this handle, in the order they completed.
  ///
  /// Through *this* handle: a builder answers a new filesystem and with it a
  /// new log, and so does `readOnly`, so the calls a test reads back are the
  /// calls made through the value it put in the context. Reading the
  /// environment back is not a call — `read`, `snapshot` and `calls` itself are
  /// not in it — and a write that was refused is one, because a refusal is an
  /// answer to something that was asked. A call the plan failed is one too.
  export fn calls(self): [FsCall] {
    fsCalls(self.handle)
  }
  /// The plan's answer for a call about to be made, recorded and consumed.
  ///
  /// The log is what counts the matching calls, which is what makes
  /// `failsOnCall`'s `nth` a number the test can check for itself: it is this
  /// call's position among the matching entries `calls()` will report. The empty
  /// plan is answered without reading the log at all, which is every test that
  /// names no fault.
  fn planned(self, call: FsCall): Option<IoError> {
    if (self.plan.len() == 0) {
      .None
    } else {
      let nth = countingFsCalls(fsCalls(self.handle), call, 0) + 1;
      match (chosenFsFault(self.handle, self.plan, call, nth, 0)) {
        .None => .None,
        .Some(error) => {
          let _ = noteFsCall(self.handle, call.name, call.path, call.body);
          .Some(error)
        },
      }
    }
  }
}

/// The first fault in the plan this call matches and this position satisfies,
/// marked as fired.
///
/// The first, so a plan is read in the order it was written. Every entry sees
/// the same `nth` — the count of matching *calls*, not of the calls this entry
/// answered — so a plan of `failsOnCall(1, a)` and `failsOnCall(2, b)` over one
/// call fails the first with `a` and the second with `b`.
fn chosenFsFault(
  handle: I64,
  plan: [FsFault],
  call: FsCall,
  nth: Int,
  i: Int,
): Option<IoError> {
  match (plan.get(i)) {
    .None => .None,
    .Some(fault) => {
      if (fault.call == call && (fault.nth == 0 || fault.nth == nth)) {
        let _ = noteFault(handle, i);
        .Some(fault.error)
      } else {
        chosenFsFault(handle, plan, call, nth, i + 1)
      }
    },
  }
}

/// How many entries of the log are this call, which is how many matching calls
/// have already been made.
fn countingFsCalls(log: [FsCall], call: FsCall, i: Int): Int {
  match (log.get(i)) {
    .None => 0,
    .Some(seen) => {
      let here = if (seen == call) { 1 } else { 0 };
      here + countingFsCalls(log, call, i + 1)
    },
  }
}

/// Tells the runner what each fault of the plan reads like in a failure
/// message, in the order the plan lists them.
///
/// One call per entry rather than one call with the list, because the list is a
/// list of Buri values and what crosses is text and two numbers — the whole of
/// what a message needs. Walking it here costs one row per fault at the moment
/// a test declares the plan, which is once per double.
fn describeFsPlan(handle: I64, plan: [FsFault], i: Int): () {
  match (plan.get(i)) {
    .None => (),
    .Some(fault) => {
      let _ = addFsFault(handle, fault.call.name, fault.call.path, fault.call.body);
      let _ = faultFails(handle, fault.nth, ioCode(fault.error), ioPayload(fault.error));
      describeFsPlan(handle, plan, i + 1)
    },
  }
}

/// An `IoError`'s index in its own declaration order, which `core/effect` states
/// is the ABI — `methodCode`'s rule, for the other enum this module hands over.
fn ioCode(error: IoError): Int {
  match (error) {
    .NotFound => 0,
    .PermissionDenied => 1,
    .ReadOnly => 2,
    .AlreadyExists => 3,
    .NotADirectory => 4,
    .CrossDevice => 5,
    .Other(_) => 6,
  }
}

/// The text `IoError.Other` carries, and the empty string for the six variants
/// that carry nothing.
fn ioPayload(error: IoError): Str {
  match (error) {
    .Other(message) => message,
    _ => "",
  }
}

/// A fresh, empty, writable filesystem, and the handle that names it.
fn newFs(): I64;

/// This view's files with these written over them, in a store of its own, under
/// this view's attenuation and this view's plan.
fn fsFiles(handle: I64, entries: [(Str, Str)]): I64;

/// The byte twin of `fsFiles`.
fn fsFilesBytes(handle: I64, entries: [(Str, [U8])]): I64;

/// A second view onto the same store, refusing every write.
fn fsReadOnly(handle: I64): I64;

/// A view onto the same store with a fresh, empty plan, retiring the plan this
/// one was using — `faults` replaces, so the promise it replaced goes with it.
fn fsWithPlan(handle: I64): I64;

/// The call half of one fault, as a failure message spells it: the `FsCall`'s
/// three fields.
///
/// Two rows for one entry, and the split is the frame-threaded backend's:
/// `stencil/abi.rs`'s `MAX_INT_ARGS` is ten, and a `Str` is three of them
/// (`lib.rs` §2 rule 1), so a row carrying a call *and* an error would be
/// fifteen. `faultFails` is the other half and is shared with the network,
/// which is what the split turned out to be worth.
fn addFsFault(handle: I64, name: Str, path: Str, body: Str): ();

/// The failure half of the fault just added: the call number `failsOnCall`
/// named or zero for every call, and the error as its variant index and the
/// text it carries.
fn faultFails(handle: I64, nth: Int, code: Int, payload: Str): ();

/// The plan entry at `index` has fired, so its promise is kept.
fn noteFault(handle: I64, index: Int): ();

/// One call, on the path that did not reach the row that would have recorded
/// it.
fn noteFsCall(handle: I64, name: Str, path: Str, body: Str): ();

/// The read-back, without the effect.
fn fsRead(handle: I64, path: Str): Result<Str, IoError>;

/// Every file, as text, sorted by path.
fn fsSnapshot(handle: I64): [(Str, Str)];

/// Every call made through this handle, in the order they completed.
fn fsCalls(handle: I64): [FsCall];

/// The eleven methods of `Fs`, as rows over a handle.
///
/// Free functions rather than methods on `TestFs`, because a `TestFs` is a
/// handle *and* a plan and an argument is flattened into its leaves: a row
/// taking `self` would be handed three values where it expects one.
/// `TestNet.calls` is the same shape, and landed for the same reason.
fn fsReadFile(handle: I64, path: Str): Result<Str, IoError>;
fn fsWriteFile(handle: I64, path: Str, body: Str): Result<(), IoError>;
fn fsFileExists(handle: I64, path: Str): Bool;
fn fsReadDir(handle: I64, path: Str): Result<[Str], IoError>;
fn fsReadFileBytes(handle: I64, path: Str): Result<[U8], IoError>;
fn fsWriteFileBytes(handle: I64, path: Str, body: [U8]): Result<(), IoError>;
fn fsAppendFile(handle: I64, path: Str, body: [U8]): Result<(), IoError>;
fn fsRenameFile(handle: I64, source: Str, destination: Str): Result<(), IoError>;
fn fsRemoveFile(handle: I64, path: Str): Result<(), IoError>;
fn fsMakeDir(handle: I64, path: Str): Result<(), IoError>;
fn fsSyncFile(handle: I64, path: Str): Result<(), IoError>;

// --- Network ------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Refuses every request, until a test says what to answer.
///
/// The default is `.Err(.Refused)` and not a stub `200`: a test that reaches
/// the network by accident should say so at its assertion rather than pass on
/// an answer nobody wrote. `core/testing/context`'s `noNet()` is this double
/// with the refusal made permanent; `net()` is the same starting point with a
/// way forward.
///
/// ## The one double whose configuration is not a handle
///
/// Every other type in this module is a newtype over an `I64` naming
/// runner-side state, because its state is *state* — a transcript that grows,
/// a clock that advances, a filesystem that is written to. A responder is not
/// state, it is **behaviour**, and behaviour is the one thing the runner
/// cannot hold:
///
///  * A function value is `{ code, env }` (`design/native/VALUE-MODEL.md` §7).
///    For the archive to invoke one it would have to call back into
///    monomorphized Buri with a `Request` it had rebuilt itself — a calling
///    convention neither `cli/runtime` nor `backend/js/runtime.js` has, and one
///    no other entry needs.
///  * The answer would have to cross as a `Result<Response, NetError>`, and
///    that is the wall `host.HostNet.fetch` already stands at: `Ret::Res` names
///    the error variant by index and `cli/runtime/lib.rs` §2.1 restricts that
///    variant to carrying no fields, while `NetError` carries a `Str` on
///    `BadUrl` and on `Transport`. Both runtime tables say so where that key is
///    listed as absent.
///
/// So `fetch` is a Buri body that calls the responder directly, and neither
/// table names it. What makes that safe rather than a hole is SPEC 10.6: a
/// lambda may not capture an effect-carrying value, so a
/// `fn(Request) => Result<Response, NetError>` is a pure function of the
/// request and of nothing else — which is exactly what a fake server is.
///
/// The consequence for whoever writes one: a responder cannot take `ctx`, so it
/// cannot call `http.text(ctx, ...)` inside itself. It can build a `Response`
/// literal, because a list literal needs no context; and it can **capture** a
/// `Response` the test built before it, because a response is plain data. Both
/// spellings are in `docs/build/testing.md`.
///
/// It carries a handle as well, and that is not a contradiction: the responder
/// is the behaviour and the handle names the **log**, which is state like any
/// other. `calls()` reads it. The third field is the **fault plan**, which is in
/// the value for the second of the two reasons above read once more — a
/// `NetError` carries a `Str` on two variants, so the plan's errors could not
/// come back out of a row.
export struct TestNet {
  handle: I64,
  responder: fn(Request) => Result<Response, NetError>,
  plan: [NetFault],
}

impl Net for TestNet {
  /// The plan first, the responder second, and the log last.
  ///
  /// The plan first because a failure the test asked for is not a failure of
  /// the responder's: a request the plan fails never reaches it, which is what
  /// makes `respond` and `faults` two answers to two different questions rather
  /// than two answers to one. The log last for `calls()`'s reason — a call is
  /// recorded on the way out — and a failed request is a request, so both paths
  /// record.
  fn fetch(self, request: Request): Result<Response, NetError> {
    let answer: Result<Response, NetError> = match (self.planned(fetch(request))) {
      .Some(error) => .Err(error),
      .None => {
        let respond = self.responder;
        respond(request)
      },
    };
    let _ = recordFetch(
      self.handle,
      methodCode(request.method),
      request.url,
      request.headers,
      request.body,
    );
    answer
  }
}

export fn net(): TestNet {
  TestNet { handle: newNet(), responder: fn(_request) => .Err(.Refused), plan: [] }
}

/// A fresh, empty log, and the handle that names it.
fn newNet(): I64;

/// A fresh, empty log carrying this one's plan, for the builder that answers a
/// new network without changing what it promised.
fn netRebind(handle: I64): I64;

/// A fresh, empty log with a fresh, empty plan, retiring the plan this handle
/// was using.
fn netWithPlan(handle: I64): I64;

/// The call half of one fault of a network's plan. The URL and not the whole
/// request — matching is `NetCall`'s `Eq` and reads every field, and a message
/// that named every header would be a paragraph. `faultFails` is the other
/// half, shared with the filesystem.
fn addNetFault(handle: I64, url: Str): ();

/// One request, recorded after the responder has answered it.
///
/// The request arrives in pieces because that is what crossing costs: a
/// `Method` is its variant index by the time the runner sees it (`methodCode`),
/// and the runner puts the four of them back together on the way out. It is the
/// same four values `core/host`'s real `fetch` hands over, in the same order.
fn recordFetch(
  handle: I64,
  method: Int,
  url: Str,
  headers: [Header],
  body: [U8],
): ();

/// A `Method`'s index in its own declaration order, which `core/effect` states
/// is the ABI. This is the one place in this module that spells it out.
fn methodCode(method: Method): Int {
  match (method) {
    .Get => 0,
    .Head => 1,
    .Post => 2,
    .Put => 3,
    .Patch => 4,
    .Delete => 5,
    .Options => 6,
  }
}

/// A `NetError`'s index in its own declaration order — `ioCode`'s rule, for the
/// error this double fails with.
fn netCode(error: NetError): Int {
  match (error) {
    .Timeout => 0,
    .Refused => 1,
    .BadUrl(_) => 2,
    .Transport(_) => 3,
    .Aborted => 4,
  }
}

/// The text `NetError`'s two payload-carrying variants carry, and the empty
/// string for the three that carry nothing.
fn netPayload(error: NetError): Str {
  match (error) {
    .BadUrl(message) => message,
    .Transport(message) => message,
    _ => "",
  }
}

impl TestNet {
  /// A **new** network answering through this function, which is handed every
  /// request the code under test makes and either answers it or fails it.
  ///
  /// One responder, not a routing table: a responder that answers two URLs
  /// differently matches on `request.path()` — or on `request.method`, or on
  /// `request.header(...)` — and a test that wanted a table would have to learn
  /// a second vocabulary to say what `match` already says. `respond` therefore
  /// **replaces** rather than composing: two responders for one request have no
  /// answer to which of them wins.
  export fn respond(
    self,
    responder: fn(Request) => Result<Response, NetError>,
  ): TestNet {
    TestNet { handle: netRebind(self.handle), responder: responder, plan: self.plan }
  }
  /// A **new** network failing the requests this plan names — and a promise
  /// that every one of them is made.
  ///
  /// `TestFs::faults` states the whole rule and it is the same rule here:
  /// matching is `NetCall`'s derived `Eq`, so two requests differing in a header
  /// are two calls and a fault names one of them; `fails` fails every matching
  /// request and `failsOnCall(n, e)` the `n`th; a failed request never reaches
  /// the responder and is still in `calls()`; and a fault whose request is never
  /// made fails the test.
  export fn faults(self, plan: [NetFault]): TestNet {
    let handle = netWithPlan(self.handle);
    let _ = describeNetPlan(handle, plan, 0);
    TestNet { handle: handle, responder: self.responder, plan: plan }
  }
  /// Every request made through this handle, in the order they were answered.
  ///
  /// `respond` mints a fresh log along with the network it answers, for the
  /// reason every builder in this module answers a new handle: a network that
  /// shared its receiver's log would report calls made to a different network.
  export fn calls(self): [NetCall] {
    netCalls(self.handle)
  }
  /// The plan's answer for a request about to be made, recorded and consumed.
  ///
  /// `TestFs::planned`, for the other double: the log counts the matching
  /// requests, and an empty plan is answered without reading it.
  fn planned(self, call: NetCall): Option<NetError> {
    if (self.plan.len() == 0) {
      .None
    } else {
      let nth = countingNetCalls(netCalls(self.handle), call, 0) + 1;
      chosenNetFault(self.handle, self.plan, call, nth, 0)
    }
  }
}

/// `chosenFsFault`, for the network. The two are one rule written at two types:
/// a `Call` is compared by the `Eq` it derives, and there is no `Fault<C, E>` to
/// write it once with, because a `calls()` answering one list cannot hold two
/// error types.
fn chosenNetFault(
  handle: I64,
  plan: [NetFault],
  call: NetCall,
  nth: Int,
  i: Int,
): Option<NetError> {
  match (plan.get(i)) {
    .None => .None,
    .Some(fault) => {
      if (fault.call == call && (fault.nth == 0 || fault.nth == nth)) {
        let _ = noteFault(handle, i);
        .Some(fault.error)
      } else {
        chosenNetFault(handle, plan, call, nth, i + 1)
      }
    },
  }
}

/// How many entries of the log are this request.
fn countingNetCalls(log: [NetCall], call: NetCall, i: Int): Int {
  match (log.get(i)) {
    .None => 0,
    .Some(seen) => {
      let here = if (seen == call) { 1 } else { 0 };
      here + countingNetCalls(log, call, i + 1)
    },
  }
}

/// `describeFsPlan`, for the network.
fn describeNetPlan(handle: I64, plan: [NetFault], i: Int): () {
  match (plan.get(i)) {
    .None => (),
    .Some(fault) => {
      let request = fault.call.0;
      let _ = addNetFault(handle, request.url);
      let _ = faultFails(handle, fault.nth, netCode(fault.error), netPayload(fault.error));
      describeNetPlan(handle, plan, i + 1)
    },
  }
}

/// The log itself, read by the handle rather than by the value that names it.
///
/// `TestNet` is the one double whose value is not a bare handle — the responder
/// is its second field — and an argument is flattened into its leaves, so a
/// method taking `self` would hand the runner a closure it has no use for and
/// could not name. Every other `calls()` is a row; this one is a Buri body over
/// a row that takes the handle.
fn netCalls(handle: I64): [NetCall];

// --- Clock --------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Starts at zero and advances only when the test advances it. `sleepMillis`
/// advances it without sleeping, which is the whole point of a test clock.
export struct TestClock(I64);
impl Clock for TestClock {
  fn nowMillis(self): I64;
  fn sleepMillis(self, millis: Int): ();
}
export fn clock(): TestClock;

impl TestClock {
  /// A **new** clock, at that instant. The one it was called on is untouched,
  /// so a test holding both holds two independent clocks.
  export fn at(self, millis: Int): TestClock;
}

// --- Randomness ---------------------------------------------------------------

/// Seeded — at zero unless a test says otherwise — so a failure reproduces.
export struct TestRand(I64);
impl Rand for TestRand {
  fn nextInt(self, lo: Int, hi: Int): Int;
  fn nextFloat(self): Float;
}
export fn rand(): TestRand;

impl TestRand {
  /// A **new** generator, at that seed and drawing from the start of its
  /// sequence.
  export fn seed(self, n: Int): TestRand;
}

// --- Environment --------------------------------------------------------------

/// Empty: no variables and no arguments, whatever the host happens to have.
export struct TestEnv(I64);
impl Env for TestEnv {
  fn variable(self, name: Str): Option<Str>;
  fn args(self): [Str];
}
export fn env(): TestEnv;

impl TestEnv {
  /// A **new** environment with these variables and this one's arguments, so
  /// the two builders compose in either order. The last binding of a name
  /// wins.
  export fn variables(self, vars: [(Str, Str)]): TestEnv;
  /// A **new** environment with these arguments and this one's variables.
  export fn arguments(self, args: [Str]): TestEnv;
}

// --- Process ------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Absorbs the exit rather than taking it. A test that ended the process would
/// take every block after it with it, and the runner would report a suite that
/// stopped rather than a function that exited.
///
/// ## The double with nothing to read back
///
/// `proc()` has no readers, and that is the whole of it: what a test asserts
/// about a function that exits is that the *test* carried on — which the
/// assertions after the call say, and which no method on this type could say
/// better. An exit code kept where nothing can read it would be state held for
/// its own sake, so it is not held: `exitWith` absorbs the call and answers
/// `()`.
///
/// That makes this the second double with no runner-side state and no row in
/// either runtime table, for the plainer of `TestNet`'s two reasons. The handle
/// names nothing, the way `TestAlloc`'s does — and, as both native backends say
/// where they open-code that one, zero is as good a name as any.
export struct TestProc(I64);
impl Proc for TestProc {
  fn exitWith(self, code: Int): () {
    let _ = code;
  }
}

export fn proc(): TestProc {
  TestProc(0)
}

// --- The call log -------------------------------------------------------------
//
// One record per double, and one constructor per method. A `Call` is what was
// asked and never what came back: the answer is the return value the test
// already has, and a log that carried it would be a second, weaker way to
// assert on it.

/// One call to a `TestFs`, as `calls()` reports it and as a test writes it
/// down.
///
/// A record with private fields, so the constructors below are the only way to
/// build one and a call in a test reads as the call the code under test made.
/// `derive Eq` is what an assertion compares — two calls differ as soon as an
/// argument does — and `derive Show` is what a failing one prints, because
/// `assert.eq` renders both sides through the generated `Show`.
///
/// One record rather than an enum of eleven variants: every method of `Fs`
/// takes a path and at most one thing more, so `path` is the first argument and
/// `body` the second — the text written, `renameFile`'s destination, or the
/// empty string where the method takes only a path. Octets are recorded as
/// **the text they spell**, which is `snapshot()`'s rule and is here for
/// `snapshot()`'s reason.
export struct FsCall {
  name: Str,
  path: Str,
  body: Str,
}

derive Eq, Show for FsCall;

/// A `readFile(path)`, as a call.
export fn readFile(path: Str): FsCall {
  FsCall { name: "readFile", path: path, body: "" }
}

/// A `writeFile(path, body)`, as a call.
export fn writeFile(path: Str, body: Str): FsCall {
  FsCall { name: "writeFile", path: path, body: body }
}

/// A `fileExists(path)`, as a call. It cannot fail, and it is still something
/// the code under test asked the filesystem.
export fn fileExists(path: Str): FsCall {
  FsCall { name: "fileExists", path: path, body: "" }
}

/// A `readDir(path)`, as a call.
export fn readDir(path: Str): FsCall {
  FsCall { name: "readDir", path: path, body: "" }
}

/// A `readFileBytes(path)`, as a call. The same path `readFile` would name and
/// a different call, because a program that asked for octets asked for octets.
export fn readFileBytes(path: Str): FsCall {
  FsCall { name: "readFileBytes", path: path, body: "" }
}

/// A `writeFileBytes(path, body)`, as a call.
export fn writeFileBytes(path: Str, body: [U8]): FsCall {
  FsCall { name: "writeFileBytes", path: path, body: spelled(body) }
}

/// An `appendFile(path, body)`, as a call.
export fn appendFile(path: Str, body: [U8]): FsCall {
  FsCall { name: "appendFile", path: path, body: spelled(body) }
}

/// A `renameFile(source, destination)`, as a call. The source is the path and
/// the destination is the second argument, as it is in the call itself.
export fn renameFile(source: Str, destination: Str): FsCall {
  FsCall { name: "renameFile", path: source, body: destination }
}

/// A `removeFile(path)`, as a call.
export fn removeFile(path: Str): FsCall {
  FsCall { name: "removeFile", path: path, body: "" }
}

/// A `makeDir(path)`, as a call.
export fn makeDir(path: Str): FsCall {
  FsCall { name: "makeDir", path: path, body: "" }
}

/// A `syncFile(path)`, as a call.
export fn syncFile(path: Str): FsCall {
  FsCall { name: "syncFile", path: path, body: "" }
}

/// The text these octets spell, exactly as `readFile` reads back what
/// `writeFileBytes` wrote.
///
/// Lossy, and lossy on purpose: a log records what a program asked to write,
/// and a fixture that is not text is still a call that was made. It is the
/// runner's decode rather than `core/bytes`' because a constructor takes no
/// context — a test writing a call down is not performing an effect.
fn spelled(body: [U8]): Str;

/// One request made through a `TestNet`.
///
/// The whole request, because `Net` has one method and it takes one value:
/// there is nothing to project away, and two requests that differ in a header
/// are two calls. `Request` derives `Eq` and `Show` itself, so this one is
/// those two derives wearing a name that says which double it came from.
export struct NetCall(Request);

derive Eq, Show for NetCall;

/// A `fetch(request)`, as a call.
export fn fetch(request: Request): NetCall {
  NetCall(request)
}

/// One read made through a `TestStdin`.
///
/// `count` is the number of octets `readBytes` asked for, and zero for
/// `readLine`, which asks for a line and names no number.
export struct StdinCall {
  name: Str,
  count: Int,
}

derive Eq, Show for StdinCall;

/// A `readLine()`, as a call.
export fn readLine(): StdinCall {
  StdinCall { name: "readLine", count: 0 }
}

/// A `readBytes(n)`, as a call.
export fn readBytes(n: Int): StdinCall {
  StdinCall { name: "readBytes", count: n }
}

// --- The fault plan -----------------------------------------------------------
//
// A plan is a list of faults and a fault is a call and an error. Success comes
// from the environment — `files`, `respond` — and failure comes from here, which
// is what keeps the two readable side by side: a fixture says what is there and
// a plan says what breaks.
//
// One `Fault` record per double, as there is one `Call` record per double, and
// for the same reason: the note's `Call<Result<R, E>>` cannot be taken
// literally, because a `calls()` answering one list cannot hold eleven result
// types and a phantom parameter would have to be erased before a log could be
// written. A double's error type is fixed by the double, so `FsCall::fails`
// takes an `IoError` and `NetCall::fails` takes a `NetError` and neither needs a
// type parameter at all.
//
// Neither record derives anything. A `Call` derives `Eq` because a plan matches
// with it and `Show` because `assert.eq` renders with it; a `Fault` is matched
// by its call and printed by the runner from the pieces `addFsFault` sends, so
// deriving here would generate two functions per program that nothing calls.

/// One planned failure of a `TestFs`.
///
/// `nth` is the call number `failsOnCall` named, counted from one over the
/// matching calls, and **zero for `fails`**, which is every call. Zero rather
/// than an `Option<Int>` because zero is not a call number — there is no zeroth
/// call — so the two spellings cannot be confused and the record stays flat.
export struct FsFault {
  call: FsCall,
  error: IoError,
  nth: Int,
}

impl FsCall {
  /// This call fails with this error, every time it is made.
  export fn fails(self, error: IoError): FsFault {
    FsFault { call: self, error: error, nth: 0 }
  }
  /// The `nth` matching call fails with this error, counted from one, and every
  /// other one is answered by the filesystem.
  ///
  /// The *matching* call: a plan that fails the second `appendFile("wal", b)`
  /// counts appends of `b` to `wal` and not calls in general, so a read between
  /// two writes does not move the number.
  export fn failsOnCall(self, nth: Int, error: IoError): FsFault {
    FsFault { call: self, error: error, nth: nth }
  }
}

/// One planned failure of a `TestNet`. `FsFault`'s two fields at the network's
/// two types.
export struct NetFault {
  call: NetCall,
  error: NetError,
  nth: Int,
}

impl NetCall {
  /// This request fails with this error, every time it is made — and it never
  /// reaches the responder.
  export fn fails(self, error: NetError): NetFault {
    NetFault { call: self, error: error, nth: 0 }
  }
  /// The `nth` matching request fails, counted from one. The retry a test is
  /// about to assert on is `failsOnCall(1, .Timeout)` and a responder.
  export fn failsOnCall(self, nth: Int, error: NetError): NetFault {
    NetFault { call: self, error: error, nth: nth }
  }
}

// --- Tasks --------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Runs every task, one at a time, in an order the test chooses — and by
/// default in the order the items are written.
///
/// `Tasks.parallel` promises its *results* in the items' order and promises
/// nothing about the order the work happens in, which is the one thing about a
/// concurrent program a test cannot otherwise pin down. This double makes that
/// order a value: `tasks()` runs the tasks in program order, `anyOrder()` runs
/// them in one seeded order, and `everyOrder()` runs the whole `test` body once
/// per completion order. Nothing here is concurrent — a double that raced would
/// be the thing it exists to remove — so a task runs to completion before the
/// next one starts, and `calls()` reports them in that order.
///
/// ## The seed is the order's own number
///
/// There are `n!` orders of `n` tasks, and a seed is which of them, counted
/// from zero in the order the orders themselves sort in: `seed(0)` is program
/// order, `seed(1)` swaps the last two, and the last seed is the reverse. So a
/// seed **replays** rather than merely re-randomising: `everyOrder`'s fourth
/// run and `seed(3)` are the same order, which is what lets a failure report
/// name one line a reader can paste back. A seed past the last one wraps, so a
/// number derived from a program's content is a legal seed at every length.
///
/// `anyOrder()` with no seed is the order **this suite's own content** names:
/// the runner derives it from the suite's action key, the hash of every source
/// in its closure that the result cache is already keyed on. So the order a
/// suite schedules in changes exactly when the verdict that order produced
/// stops being reusable, and never on a run that changed nothing. It is
/// deliberately not a random one: a suite whose result changed between two runs
/// of the same program could not be cached, and a failure nobody can reproduce
/// is a failure nobody fixes. A block that means one particular order says
/// `seed(n)`; the failure report names the seed that replays the one it hit.
///
/// ## A bare handle, where `TestFs` and `TestNet` carry two things
///
/// Those two hold their fault plan in the program because a plan carries an
/// `IoError` or a `NetError`, and `cli/runtime/lib.rs` §2.1 cannot hand an
/// error variant with a field back across a row. A task has no error to carry —
/// `parallel` answers `[B]` and every `B` comes from the closure — so a fault
/// here is an *index*, a count and a sentence, all three of which cross. The
/// runner therefore does the matching as well as the promising, and this value
/// is the handle and nothing else.
export struct TestTasks(I64);

impl Tasks for TestTasks {
  /// Every task once, in the order this double schedules them, and the results
  /// in the items' order.
  ///
  /// The runtime drives the walk, through the same closure trampoline
  /// `host.HostTasks.parallel` uses (`backend/intrinsic_keys.rs`'s `step_call`)
  /// and for the reason that mechanism exists: this double's job is the
  /// *order*, and choosing one is the runtime's. So the double runs the **same**
  /// boundary the real implementation runs, which is what makes a test of a
  /// program through this an honest test of the program through that.
  ///
  /// `ctx` is the caller's whole context and is what every step is handed —
  /// `Tasks`' own promise, and the same parameter the real implementation
  /// takes. A `Buri` body could supply it too (`SerialTasks` in the
  /// conformance corpus does); what it could not supply is the order.
  fn parallel<C, A, B>(self, ctx: C, items: [A], f: fn(C, Int, A) => B): [B];
}

/// Program order, no faults, and a fresh log.
export fn tasks(): TestTasks;

impl TestTasks {
  /// A **new** scheduler running its tasks in one seeded order.
  ///
  /// The default seed is the reverse of program order; `seed(n)` names another.
  /// One order per run of the body, not one per `parallel`: two fan-outs of the
  /// same length in one block are scheduled the same way, so a test that asserts
  /// on both is asserting about one decision rather than two.
  export fn anyOrder(self): TestTasks;
  /// A **new** scheduler that runs the whole `test` body once per completion
  /// order — `n!` times for a `parallel` over `n` items.
  ///
  /// The body, not the fan-out: a task's effects are the point, and re-running
  /// only the loop would re-run them against a filesystem the last order had
  /// already written to. Every run therefore builds its own doubles from the
  /// same lines, which is what makes the runs independent and what makes the
  /// assertion at the end of the body an assertion about *every* order.
  ///
  /// Six items are 720 runs of the body and seven are 5040, so this refuses
  /// above six and says so. A fan-out that wide is `anyOrder`'s question.
  ///
  /// The first failing order ends the block, because a failed assertion is an
  /// abort and there is nothing to catch (SPEC 6.10) — so the report names the
  /// order that failed and the seed that replays it, and the orders after it do
  /// not run.
  export fn everyOrder(self): TestTasks;
  /// A **new** scheduler at that seed — the order's own number, counted from
  /// zero, wrapping past the last.
  ///
  /// This is `anyOrder` with the choice named, and it is what a failure report
  /// prints for a reader to paste back: `seed(3)` is `everyOrder`'s fourth run,
  /// exactly.
  export fn seed(self, n: Int): TestTasks;
  /// A **new** scheduler failing the tasks this plan names — and a promise that
  /// every one of them is reached.
  ///
  /// A task has no error channel to answer through, so a task the plan fails is
  /// a task that ends the program — which is what a task that died would do to
  /// the run for real. A fault is therefore a call and a **reason**, and the
  /// block fails with that reason where a real one would have failed with a
  /// stack. The tasks scheduled before it have run and had their effects; the
  /// ones after it have not started.
  ///
  /// The rules are `TestFs::faults`' rules: `task(k).fails(why)` fails that task
  /// every time it is reached and `task(k).failsOnCall(n, why)` the `n`th time,
  /// counted from one over the matching tasks — which counts `parallel` calls,
  /// since one call reaches each index once. A fault whose task is never
  /// reached fails the test. `faults` replaces rather than composing, and
  /// retires the plan it replaced.
  export fn faults(self, plan: [TaskFault]): TestTasks {
    let planned = self.replan();
    let _ = describeTaskPlan(planned, plan, 0);
    planned
  }
  /// Every task this scheduler ran, in the order they completed.
  ///
  /// The order the *work* happened in, which is what this double exists to
  /// choose and the only place it is observable: the result list is in the
  /// items' order whatever this says. A task the plan failed is not here,
  /// because it never completed — and the block it ended has no assertion left
  /// to read this with anyway.
  export fn calls(self): [TaskCall];
  /// Which run of the `test` body this is, counted from one.
  ///
  /// One for a block that is not being re-run, so a test that reads it under
  /// `tasks()` or `anyOrder()` reads the truth rather than a special case. Under
  /// `everyOrder` it is the number of the order this run is using, plus one —
  /// `seed(runs() - 1)` is this run's order.
  ///
  /// The answer is the **block**'s rather than this handle's: the body's runs
  /// are the runner's bookkeeping, and a block with two schedulers in it is
  /// still one block being run again. It is a method because that is how a test
  /// reaches it, not because the handle decides it.
  export fn runs(self): Int;
  /// How many runs of the body there will be: `n!` under `everyOrder` once a
  /// `parallel` has said what `n` is, and one otherwise.
  ///
  /// One before the first `parallel`, because until then nothing knows how many
  /// tasks there are to order.
  export fn orders(self): Int;
  /// A **new** scheduler with a fresh, empty plan and this one's ordering,
  /// retiring the plan it was using.
  fn replan(self): TestTasks;
  /// One entry of a plan: which task, on which call, and why — the three
  /// pieces a failure message is assembled from, and the three the runner
  /// matches on.
  fn addFault(self, index: Int, nth: Int, reason: Str): ();
}

/// Hands the runner each entry of a plan as it is installed.
///
/// `describeFsPlan`'s walk. It is a walk here rather than a list crossing whole
/// because a `[TaskFault]` is a list of records holding a `Str`, and one row per
/// entry is the shape the other two doubles already use.
fn describeTaskPlan(ctx: TestTasks, plan: [TaskFault], i: Int): () {
  match (plan.get(i)) {
    .None => (),
    .Some(fault) => {
      let _ = ctx.addFault(fault.call.index, fault.nth, fault.reason);
      describeTaskPlan(ctx, plan, i + 1)
    },
  }
}

/// One task of one `parallel`, named by its index — as `calls()` reports it and
/// as a plan names it.
///
/// The index and not the item: a task is identified by where it is in the
/// fan-out, which is the one thing every task of every `parallel` has and the
/// only thing the effect's signature promises about it (`f` is handed the
/// index). An item would have to be `Show`n to be recorded and `Eq` to be
/// matched, and `parallel` bounds neither.
export struct TaskCall {
  index: Int,
}

derive Eq, Show for TaskCall;

/// The task at that index, as a call.
export fn task(index: Int): TaskCall {
  TaskCall { index: index }
}

/// One planned failure of a `TestTasks`. `FsFault`'s three fields, with a reason
/// where the filesystem has an error: a task answers `B` and has no error
/// channel, so what a fault carries is what the failure will say.
export struct TaskFault {
  call: TaskCall,
  reason: Str,
  nth: Int,
}

impl TaskCall {
  /// This task fails with this reason, every time it is reached.
  export fn fails(self, reason: Str): TaskFault {
    TaskFault { call: self, reason: reason, nth: 0 }
  }
  /// The `nth` time this task is reached it fails, counted from one over the
  /// matching tasks — which is once per `parallel`, so `n` is the number of the
  /// fan-out rather than of the task.
  export fn failsOnCall(self, nth: Int, reason: Str): TaskFault {
    TaskFault { call: self, reason: reason, nth: nth }
  }
}
